WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.338
Chapter four. Cowries: the money that got too heavy to carry.

00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:11.807
For centuries, much of West Africa used a small seashell as money: the cowrie.

00:00:11.807 --> 00:00:15.149
Most came from the Maldives, in the Indian Ocean.

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:20.727
One shell bought very little, so people counted them. Forty to a string.

00:00:20.727 --> 00:00:22.397
Two thousand to a head.

00:00:22.748 --> 00:00:27.154
West African traders wanted shells: their markets ran on them.

00:00:27.154 --> 00:00:32.129
So European ships brought shells by the ton, to buy enslaved people...

00:00:32.129 --> 00:00:33.550
and later, palm oil.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:38.042
In eighteen forty-five, a Hamburg firm found a shortcut.

00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:42.553
In Zanzibar, local traders sold a bigger cowrie for far less.

00:00:42.553 --> 00:00:44.993
The firm shipped some to Dahomey.

00:00:45.343 --> 00:00:50.941
German and French firms followed. Coastal merchants bought them: they were cheap.

00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:54.397
By one estimate, sixteen billion shells poured in.

00:00:55.247 --> 00:01:01.065
In Dahomey, a silver dollar cost about two thousand shells in the eighteen forties.

00:01:01.065 --> 00:01:04.009
By the eighteen eighties: twenty thousand.

00:01:04.009 --> 00:01:09.126
A family that had saved two thousand shells now held a tenth of a dollar.

00:01:09.126 --> 00:01:11.299
They hadn't spent a single one.

00:01:12.849 --> 00:01:14.672
And the money got heavy.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:21.582
Around nineteen hundred in Lagos, one small silver shilling was worth four thousand shells.

00:01:21.582 --> 00:01:25.074
A coin in your fist... or a sack on your head.

00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:31.359
Remember Ulm Hollow's bogwool? Too bulky to be money.

00:01:31.359 --> 00:01:36.492
Flood a money, and it turns into bogwool. Wren found a cave.

00:01:36.492 --> 00:01:41.540
Hamburg found a reef. Next: what if Wren... is the emperor?

